+## Session backlog
+
+A sticky session collects asks one turn at a time, and an ask that lives only in the chat gets dropped. The chat that runs n-mode, never a delegate, keeps a backlog file of the user's mid-session messages per `references/session-backlog.md`. The run's opening request goes through Interview instead. Read that file when the first message after the opening request arrives. Each such message that asks for a change or gives feedback becomes a draft row, or updates a queued row on the same work, before any other step. It becomes a task once checked against the code and the backlog: you confirm a low-risk row yourself, and a row with an open call or a high risk waits on the user while other work goes on. A message that only reorders, holds, or drops rows edits them at once instead. On each new turn while a row is open, list the open title lines with `grep -n '^- \[ \]'` on the backlog, then read in full only the rows that turn touches and their intents, when they have one, before anything else. End a reply with the title lines of the rows that changed that turn, plus any row still tagged `waiting`. When a new run starts and `backlog.md` already holds open rows, name their title lines in its first reply. Session pickup reads `<root>/.claude/worktrees/backlog.md`, when it exists, as open work at its step 2, along with that reference, and the resume note of Pause safely points at it.
